          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Pierre\",\"addressee\":\"Hélène\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Je vous aime!\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"This is a direct, truth-conditional avowal of Pierre's present love for Hélène. Its awkwardly formulaic delivery complicates sincerity and depth but does not change its dominant linguistic mode as an avowal; Pierre's immediately preceding thought also presents the state as true.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Теперь уж поздно, всё кончено; да и я люблю ее», подумал Пьер.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not itself promise marriage, propose, or specify any future conduct or obligation. The scene already represents Pierre and Hélène as engaged, but that surrounding relational settlement should not be transferred onto the words “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Je vous aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Пьер молча держал руку своей невесты\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The expression is not portrayed as a reflex-like affective outburst. Pierre deliberately recalls the conventional words expected in the situation and then pronounces them, so emotional delivery does not establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"«Что-то такое особенное говорят в этих случаях», думал он, но никак не мог вспомнить, чтò такое именно говорят в этих случаях.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Besides avowing love, the utterance moderately functions as compliance with and completion of a socially expected romantic script. The narration explicitly presents Pierre as saying what one is supposed to say on such an occasion, and notes the formula's inadequate sound to him. This residual script-completion function is not itself an undertaking or an affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"— Je vous aime! — сказал он, вспомнив то, чтó нужно было говорить в этих случаях; но слова эти прозвучали так бедно, что ему стало стыдно за себя.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Pierre says this directly to Hélène while they are alone after her father and the assembled company have already treated them as an engaged couple. Hélène has just initiated a kiss. Pierre thinks that matters are already settled and that he loves her, then recalls and delivers the conventional declaration expected in such a situation; he is immediately dissatisfied with how poor the words sound.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal words contain no explicit undertaking. The surrounding scene represents a marriage engagement as already socially established and accepted: Pierre holds the hand of his fiancée, Hélène has kissed him, and her father has blessed them. That uptake belongs to the broader interaction rather than being created by “Je vous aime.”\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"I recognise the supplied passage as from Leo Tolstoy's War and Peace. The classification uses only the supplied evidence, not remembered plot facts.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supplies strong evidence of attraction and Pierre's represented belief that he loves Hélène, but his dread, social pressure, and sense of reciting an expected formula leave the depth and durability of that affection uncertain. The later marriage reported in the block does not retroactively turn this particular avowal into a promise.\"}"
        }
